1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,319
Your rent is $1,100.

2
00:00:02,319 --> 00:00:05,200
You have three days before your landlord kicks you out,

3
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:07,080
and your bank account is empty.

4
00:00:07,080 --> 00:00:08,560
What do you do?

5
00:00:08,560 --> 00:00:12,080
In October 2007, that wasn't a hypothetical question

6
00:00:12,080 --> 00:00:15,519
for Brian Chesky and his roommate, Joe Gebier.

7
00:00:15,519 --> 00:00:17,679
They were two broke design school graduates

8
00:00:17,679 --> 00:00:20,440
sharing an apartment, completely unemployed,

9
00:00:20,440 --> 00:00:22,280
and scrambling for any quick cash

10
00:00:22,280 --> 00:00:24,199
just to survive the weekend.

11
00:00:24,199 --> 00:00:27,000
Then pure luck handed them an opening.

12
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:29,239
A massive international design conference

13
00:00:29,239 --> 00:00:32,000
was flooding into town, and every single hotel room

14
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:34,679
in the city was completely sold out.

15
00:00:34,679 --> 00:00:37,600
Tourists had nowhere to sleep, so Brian and Joe

16
00:00:37,600 --> 00:00:40,280
looked at their empty floor, ran to target,

17
00:00:40,280 --> 00:00:42,560
bought three cheap blue air mattresses,

18
00:00:42,560 --> 00:00:45,119
and charged desperate designers 80 bucks a night

19
00:00:45,119 --> 00:00:48,200
to sleep on their rug, throwing in a box of pop tarts

20
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:49,439
in the morning.

21
00:00:49,439 --> 00:00:52,600
18 years later, that embarrassing emergency hustle

22
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:56,000
on the floor became a $100 billion empire.

23
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:58,479
And if you are an entrepreneur or solo builder,

24
00:00:58,479 --> 00:01:01,399
trying to start something with zero cash today,

25
00:01:01,399 --> 00:01:04,120
this journey holds the ultimate blueprint.

26
00:01:04,120 --> 00:01:05,840
Because by the end of this video,

27
00:01:05,840 --> 00:01:08,840
you are going to see the three uncensored tactical survival

28
00:01:08,840 --> 00:01:12,000
moves Brian used to hijack an entire industry,

29
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:14,439
and the fatal scaling trap you must avoid

30
00:01:14,439 --> 00:01:17,879
so your business doesn't destroy itself once it wins.

31
00:01:17,879 --> 00:01:20,760
That first weekend actually worked.

32
00:01:20,760 --> 00:01:24,400
The three designers handed over $240 in cash,

33
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:26,319
and the rent was paid.

34
00:01:26,319 --> 00:01:29,079
Brian and Joe looked at the deflated air mattresses

35
00:01:29,079 --> 00:01:33,159
on the floor and realized, wait, if complete strangers

36
00:01:33,159 --> 00:01:35,159
are willing to sleep in our living room,

37
00:01:35,159 --> 00:01:38,400
what if people could do this in every city on earth?

38
00:01:38,400 --> 00:01:40,120
There was only one problem.

39
00:01:40,120 --> 00:01:42,680
Neither of them knew how to write software code.

40
00:01:42,680 --> 00:01:45,239
They were industrial designers who drew sketches,

41
00:01:45,239 --> 00:01:46,640
not programmers.

42
00:01:46,640 --> 00:01:50,000
So they called up their former roommate, Nate Blaschartick,

43
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:51,920
a gifted software engineer,

44
00:01:51,920 --> 00:01:54,359
and convinced him to build a primitive website

45
00:01:54,359 --> 00:01:56,480
called Air Bed and Breakfast.

46
00:01:56,480 --> 00:01:59,040
The idea was simple, turn everyday homes

47
00:01:59,040 --> 00:02:01,280
into mini bed and breakfasts.

48
00:02:01,280 --> 00:02:04,319
Once the website was live in early 2008,

49
00:02:04,319 --> 00:02:06,719
they thought the hardest part was over.

50
00:02:06,719 --> 00:02:09,080
They needed cash to keep the servers running

51
00:02:09,080 --> 00:02:11,080
so they took their brand new website

52
00:02:11,080 --> 00:02:13,960
to top Silicon Valley venture capitalists.

53
00:02:13,960 --> 00:02:16,520
The meetings were an absolute disaster.

54
00:02:16,520 --> 00:02:19,159
Investors literally laughed in their faces,

55
00:02:19,159 --> 00:02:22,439
renowned venture capitalists walked out of coffee shops

56
00:02:22,439 --> 00:02:25,479
or stared at them like they were completely insane.

57
00:02:25,479 --> 00:02:28,879
Why on earth would anyone invest in an app for strangers

58
00:02:28,879 --> 00:02:32,000
to sleep on air mattresses on dirty floors?

59
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:36,479
Every single investor said no, air mattresses on dirty floors.

60
00:02:36,479 --> 00:02:38,400
Every single investor said no.

61
00:02:38,400 --> 00:02:42,759
They had a live website, zero funding and zero users.

62
00:02:42,759 --> 00:02:46,319
Over the next 10 months, the business completely flatlined.

63
00:02:46,319 --> 00:02:48,360
At the South by Southwest Festival,

64
00:02:48,360 --> 00:02:50,719
they got exactly two bookings.

65
00:02:50,719 --> 00:02:52,639
And one of them was Brian himself.

66
00:02:52,639 --> 00:02:55,520
Now, the three founders were completely broke

67
00:02:55,520 --> 00:02:57,080
and drowning in death.

68
00:02:57,080 --> 00:02:59,439
Not one credit card, not two cards,

69
00:02:59,439 --> 00:03:01,599
but entire baseball card binder albums

70
00:03:01,599 --> 00:03:03,680
filled with maxed out plastic.

71
00:03:03,680 --> 00:03:07,759
$20,000 in high interest debt, no revenue coming in

72
00:03:07,759 --> 00:03:10,960
and collection agencies calling them every single afternoon.

73
00:03:10,960 --> 00:03:12,879
How do you keep a website alive

74
00:03:12,879 --> 00:03:14,680
when you can't even afford groceries?

75
00:03:14,680 --> 00:03:17,159
Then came August 2008,

76
00:03:17,159 --> 00:03:19,919
the Democratic National Convention in Denver.

77
00:03:19,919 --> 00:03:22,159
A hundred thousand people were showing up

78
00:03:22,159 --> 00:03:26,879
and there were only 17,000 hotel rooms in the entire city.

79
00:03:26,879 --> 00:03:29,520
It was the exact same hotel crisis

80
00:03:29,520 --> 00:03:32,759
that saved them the first time, but 50 times bigger.

81
00:03:32,759 --> 00:03:36,280
The founders had zero cash left to keep their servers online.

82
00:03:36,280 --> 00:03:39,360
So they did what any rational tech founder does.

83
00:03:39,360 --> 00:03:41,599
They went into the breakfast cereal business.

84
00:03:41,599 --> 00:03:44,120
They convinced a college student in Rhode Island

85
00:03:44,120 --> 00:03:47,639
to print 1,000 custom cardboard boxes on credit.

86
00:03:47,639 --> 00:03:49,680
They folded them by hand in their kitchen,

87
00:03:49,680 --> 00:03:51,599
glued them with hot glue guns

88
00:03:51,599 --> 00:03:53,400
and filled them with cheap bulk cereal

89
00:03:53,400 --> 00:03:56,560
called Obama O's and Captain McCain's.

90
00:03:56,560 --> 00:04:01,080
At $40 a box, people thought it was a brilliant political stunt,

91
00:04:01,080 --> 00:04:02,439
but it wasn't a stunt at all.

92
00:04:02,439 --> 00:04:04,240
It was pure grocery money.

93
00:04:04,240 --> 00:04:07,120
They sold $30,000 worth of cereal,

94
00:04:07,120 --> 00:04:09,000
paid off their credit card interest

95
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:12,000
and bought themselves four more months of life.

96
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:16,000
The $30,000 from cereal bought them four months of runway,

97
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:18,439
but their website was still flatlined.

98
00:04:18,439 --> 00:04:22,680
In January 2009, they walked into an interview at Y Combinator,

99
00:04:22,680 --> 00:04:26,160
the most prestigious startup accelerator in Silicon Valley.

100
00:04:26,160 --> 00:04:29,279
The interview with founder Paul Graham was a disaster.

101
00:04:29,279 --> 00:04:31,959
Graham listened to the pitch about random strangers

102
00:04:31,959 --> 00:04:36,279
sleeping on air mattresses in private apartments and hated it.

103
00:04:36,279 --> 00:04:39,720
He told him flat out, the idea is terrible.

104
00:04:39,720 --> 00:04:41,040
People are going to get robbed.

105
00:04:41,040 --> 00:04:42,560
People are going to get murdered

106
00:04:42,560 --> 00:04:44,920
and nobody will ever pay for this.

107
00:04:44,920 --> 00:04:46,720
As they were packing up to leave,

108
00:04:46,720 --> 00:04:48,560
Brian reached into his backpack,

109
00:04:48,560 --> 00:04:51,720
pulled out an authentic box of Obama O's cereal

110
00:04:51,720 --> 00:04:53,399
and handed it across the table.

111
00:04:53,399 --> 00:04:56,480
He explained that when venture capitalists laughed at them

112
00:04:56,480 --> 00:04:58,720
and their bank accounts hit zero,

113
00:04:58,720 --> 00:05:02,639
they hand folded and glued 1,000 boxes in their kitchen

114
00:05:02,639 --> 00:05:04,040
just to keep the lights on.

115
00:05:04,040 --> 00:05:06,680
Paul Graham looked down at the cereal box,

116
00:05:06,680 --> 00:05:09,600
looked up at the three exhausted founders

117
00:05:09,600 --> 00:05:12,000
and said something that changed startup history.

118
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:16,519
If you can convince people to pay $40 for a $4 box of cereal,

119
00:05:16,519 --> 00:05:19,399
you can convince people to sleep on air beds.

120
00:05:19,399 --> 00:05:20,240
You're in.

121
00:05:20,240 --> 00:05:22,720
They got $20,000 in seed funding,

122
00:05:22,720 --> 00:05:26,399
but getting into why combinator didn't magically save them.

123
00:05:26,399 --> 00:05:28,240
Right here in March 2009,

124
00:05:28,240 --> 00:05:31,680
they made the smartest strategic pivot of their lives.

125
00:05:31,680 --> 00:05:34,480
The original name, air bed and breakfast,

126
00:05:34,480 --> 00:05:37,079
was actively suffocating the business.

127
00:05:37,079 --> 00:05:39,120
Travelers assumed they would be sleeping

128
00:05:39,120 --> 00:05:43,360
on squeaky rubber pool mattresses in a stranger's living room

129
00:05:43,360 --> 00:05:45,920
and hosts with beautiful spare bedrooms

130
00:05:45,920 --> 00:05:49,000
or empty apartments refused to list on a site

131
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:52,000
that literally had the word air bed in the title.

132
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:53,680
So in the middle of why combinator,

133
00:05:53,680 --> 00:05:55,480
they officially bought the domain

134
00:05:55,480 --> 00:05:58,240
and shortened the name down to just three letters.

135
00:05:58,240 --> 00:06:00,199
Airbnb.com.

136
00:06:00,199 --> 00:06:02,920
Dropping the words air bed and breakfast

137
00:06:02,920 --> 00:06:05,120
was the psychological masterstroke

138
00:06:05,120 --> 00:06:06,879
that transformed them from eccentric,

139
00:06:06,879 --> 00:06:10,879
florental gimmick into a real marketplace for actual homes.

140
00:06:10,879 --> 00:06:12,319
Yet even with the new name,

141
00:06:12,319 --> 00:06:14,160
growth refused a budge.

142
00:06:14,160 --> 00:06:17,759
By June 2009, the accelerator was ending

143
00:06:17,759 --> 00:06:21,800
and their weekly revenue was stuck at barely $200 a week.

144
00:06:21,800 --> 00:06:24,199
They were living in a windowless office,

145
00:06:24,199 --> 00:06:26,439
surviving on instant noodles

146
00:06:26,439 --> 00:06:30,759
and staring at a growth chart as flat as a dead man's pulse.

147
00:06:30,759 --> 00:06:33,079
Paul Graham pulled Brian and Joe aside

148
00:06:33,079 --> 00:06:34,759
and asked a simple question,

149
00:06:34,759 --> 00:06:37,000
where are your few actual customers?

150
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:40,240
Brian answered that 30 of their 40 active listings

151
00:06:40,240 --> 00:06:41,560
were in New York City.

152
00:06:41,560 --> 00:06:44,720
That was when Graham gave them the single piece of advice

153
00:06:44,720 --> 00:06:46,680
that became Silicon Valley law.

154
00:06:46,680 --> 00:06:48,160
Do things that don't scale.

155
00:06:48,160 --> 00:06:49,920
Get on a plane to New York right now.

156
00:06:49,920 --> 00:06:52,399
Go knock on every single host door.

157
00:06:52,399 --> 00:06:55,639
Brian and Joe didn't have money for plane tickets.

158
00:06:55,639 --> 00:06:57,480
So they put them on another credit card.

159
00:06:57,480 --> 00:07:00,759
They flew to Brooklyn, rented a $500 camera

160
00:07:00,759 --> 00:07:02,720
and literally walked down the streets

161
00:07:02,720 --> 00:07:04,759
knocking on their user's doors.

162
00:07:04,759 --> 00:07:06,199
They didn't try to sell anything.

163
00:07:06,199 --> 00:07:09,199
They asked hosts if they could take professional photos

164
00:07:09,199 --> 00:07:10,800
of their apartments for free.

165
00:07:10,800 --> 00:07:13,639
They sat in their kitchens, drank tea with them

166
00:07:13,639 --> 00:07:16,040
and asked what sucked about using the website.

167
00:07:16,040 --> 00:07:19,079
And right there in a tiny apartment in New York,

168
00:07:19,079 --> 00:07:21,639
the founders discovered the dirty secret

169
00:07:21,639 --> 00:07:23,920
behind why their company was failing.

170
00:07:23,920 --> 00:07:26,199
Their website didn't have a tech problem.

171
00:07:26,199 --> 00:07:27,920
It had a visual problem.

172
00:07:27,920 --> 00:07:29,879
Every listing on the site was shot

173
00:07:29,879 --> 00:07:32,560
on cheap 2008 flip phones.

174
00:07:32,560 --> 00:07:36,360
The rooms were dark, blurry and looked like crime scenes.

175
00:07:36,360 --> 00:07:38,160
Nobody wanted to stay in a room

176
00:07:38,160 --> 00:07:40,319
that looked like a basement hostage video.

177
00:07:40,319 --> 00:07:42,439
Brian took professional, bright,

178
00:07:42,439 --> 00:07:45,959
wide-angle interior photos, uploaded them that evening.

179
00:07:45,959 --> 00:07:49,160
And within 30 days, bookings in New York doubled.

180
00:07:49,160 --> 00:07:50,199
Then they tripled.

181
00:07:50,199 --> 00:07:52,040
For the first time in two years,

182
00:07:52,040 --> 00:07:54,160
revenue started rocketing up.

183
00:07:54,160 --> 00:07:55,720
The New York photography hack

184
00:07:55,720 --> 00:07:58,279
proved people actually wanted to book unique homes,

185
00:07:58,279 --> 00:07:59,959
but now they hit a new wall.

186
00:07:59,959 --> 00:08:03,120
They had no money to market the company nationwide.

187
00:08:03,120 --> 00:08:05,959
They couldn't afford expensive Google search ads

188
00:08:05,959 --> 00:08:08,800
and billboard companies wouldn't even return their calls.

189
00:08:08,800 --> 00:08:11,519
So the founders looked at the biggest online real estate

190
00:08:11,519 --> 00:08:13,920
classified site on the planet.

191
00:08:13,920 --> 00:08:18,160
Craigslist, in 2010, Craigslist had over 50 million

192
00:08:18,160 --> 00:08:21,920
active visitors looking for housing every single month.

193
00:08:21,920 --> 00:08:24,279
But they had zero public API.

194
00:08:24,279 --> 00:08:26,680
There was no official way to plug another company

195
00:08:26,680 --> 00:08:27,879
into their system.

196
00:08:27,879 --> 00:08:30,439
So Airbnb's technical team pulled off

197
00:08:30,439 --> 00:08:34,240
one of the most audacious, legally great growth hacks

198
00:08:34,240 --> 00:08:35,559
in internet history.

199
00:08:35,559 --> 00:08:39,159
They reverse engineered Craigslist form submissions.

200
00:08:39,159 --> 00:08:41,799
Whenever a host created a listing on Airbnb,

201
00:08:41,799 --> 00:08:44,000
a simple checkbox popped up.

202
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:46,879
Would you also like to post this listing to Craigslist?

203
00:08:46,879 --> 00:08:48,440
If the host clicked yes,

204
00:08:48,440 --> 00:08:51,559
a custom script automatically formatted the listing,

205
00:08:51,559 --> 00:08:54,360
bypass Craigslist anti-spam filters,

206
00:08:54,360 --> 00:08:57,720
published it to millions of Craigslist department hunters,

207
00:08:57,720 --> 00:09:00,480
and inserted a clean clickable link reading.

208
00:09:00,480 --> 00:09:04,480
Check availability and book securely on Airbnb.

209
00:09:04,480 --> 00:09:05,480
It was brilliant.

210
00:09:05,480 --> 00:09:07,799
It was completely unauthorized.

211
00:09:07,799 --> 00:09:10,879
And it siphoned millions of high intent travelers.

212
00:09:10,879 --> 00:09:14,320
Directly off Craigslist servers for exactly $0

213
00:09:14,320 --> 00:09:15,720
in marketing spend.

214
00:09:15,720 --> 00:09:19,600
By the time Craigslist realized their classified section

215
00:09:19,600 --> 00:09:22,600
was being used as a free customer acquisition funnel

216
00:09:22,600 --> 00:09:25,000
and shut it down, Airbnb was already

217
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:27,519
an unstoppable global juggernaut.

218
00:09:27,519 --> 00:09:31,360
Over the next decade, from 2011 to 2019,

219
00:09:31,360 --> 00:09:33,200
nothing could slow them down.

220
00:09:33,200 --> 00:09:36,600
Venture capital giants like Sequoia poured in hundreds

221
00:09:36,600 --> 00:09:38,320
of millions of dollars.

222
00:09:38,320 --> 00:09:41,919
The platform exploded from an air bed in San Francisco

223
00:09:41,919 --> 00:09:46,080
to seven million listings across 80,000 cities worldwide.

224
00:09:46,080 --> 00:09:49,519
Traditional hotel chains with their sterile lobbies,

225
00:09:49,519 --> 00:09:52,759
expensive room service, and stiff front desks

226
00:09:52,759 --> 00:09:54,120
were getting slaughtered.

227
00:09:54,120 --> 00:09:57,600
By two design grads who let people stay in private villas,

228
00:09:57,600 --> 00:10:00,480
rustic tree houses, and beach front-offs,

229
00:10:00,480 --> 00:10:02,440
the founders had conquered the world.

230
00:10:02,440 --> 00:10:04,879
And then March 2020 arrived.

231
00:10:04,879 --> 00:10:09,240
In literally eight weeks, the entire global travel industry

232
00:10:09,240 --> 00:10:11,679
vanished off the face of the earth.

233
00:10:11,679 --> 00:10:14,879
Airbnb's bookings collapsed by 80%.

234
00:10:14,879 --> 00:10:18,360
The company was forced to issue over $1 billion

235
00:10:18,360 --> 00:10:22,639
in cash refunds to furious travelers out of its own reserves.

236
00:10:22,639 --> 00:10:27,360
On May 5, 2020, Brian Cheskey had a hold back tears

237
00:10:27,360 --> 00:10:29,159
on a company wide video call.

238
00:10:29,159 --> 00:10:34,039
In layoff 1900 people, 25% of his workforce,

239
00:10:34,039 --> 00:10:37,320
Wall Street analysts declared the company dead in the water.

240
00:10:37,320 --> 00:10:40,440
Most founders would have declared bankruptcy right there.

241
00:10:40,440 --> 00:10:44,279
Instead, Cheskey stripped the company down to its bare metal.

242
00:10:44,279 --> 00:10:47,080
He killed expensive moonshot divisions,

243
00:10:47,080 --> 00:10:50,639
slash corporate overhead, and noticed something fascinating

244
00:10:50,639 --> 00:10:51,960
in the booking data.

245
00:10:51,960 --> 00:10:54,679
People were terrified of getting on airplanes

246
00:10:54,679 --> 00:10:57,559
to fly to crowded European capitals.

247
00:10:57,559 --> 00:10:59,879
But they were desperate to get out of their cramped city

248
00:10:59,879 --> 00:11:00,919
apartments.

249
00:11:00,919 --> 00:11:04,080
Millions of remote workers were packing their cars

250
00:11:04,080 --> 00:11:06,679
and driving two hours into the mountains

251
00:11:06,679 --> 00:11:10,879
to rent secluded cabins with fast Wi-Fi for months at a time.

252
00:11:10,879 --> 00:11:12,840
The staycation boom was born.

253
00:11:12,840 --> 00:11:16,240
By the end of the year, Airbnb wasn't just surviving.

254
00:11:16,240 --> 00:11:20,759
It was posting record cash flow on December 10, 2020,

255
00:11:20,759 --> 00:11:23,080
right in the middle of a global crisis.

256
00:11:23,080 --> 00:11:26,440
Airbnb pulled off one of the most miraculous Wall Street

257
00:11:26,440 --> 00:11:28,799
debuts in financial history.

258
00:11:28,799 --> 00:11:30,679
The stock opened on the NASDAQ,

259
00:11:30,679 --> 00:11:32,960
doubled on its very first day of trading,

260
00:11:32,960 --> 00:11:37,200
and valued the company at over $100 billion.

261
00:11:37,200 --> 00:11:39,399
The broke design school graduate, who

262
00:11:39,399 --> 00:11:42,879
is three days from an eviction notice in 2007,

263
00:11:42,879 --> 00:11:46,759
was now running the most valuable travel company on the planet.

264
00:11:46,759 --> 00:11:50,360
This is where the story stops being an inspirational poster

265
00:11:50,360 --> 00:11:53,320
for young entrepreneurs and turns it to a corporate horror

266
00:11:53,320 --> 00:11:54,159
movie.

267
00:11:54,159 --> 00:11:57,360
Because when a company reaches $100 billion,

268
00:11:57,360 --> 00:12:00,679
the original incentives warped beyond recognition.

269
00:12:00,679 --> 00:12:03,399
The everyday people who rented a spare room

270
00:12:03,399 --> 00:12:06,519
to pay their rent or meet travelers from overseas.

271
00:12:06,519 --> 00:12:08,320
They got completely pushed out.

272
00:12:08,320 --> 00:12:12,919
Instead, Wall Street private equity funds and predatory landlords

273
00:12:12,919 --> 00:12:16,159
realized they could buy up entire apartment buildings,

274
00:12:16,159 --> 00:12:20,320
kick out local families and run unlicensed commercial shadow

275
00:12:20,320 --> 00:12:21,519
hotels.

276
00:12:21,519 --> 00:12:26,360
By 2022 and 2023, you would browse Airbnb,

277
00:12:26,360 --> 00:12:30,159
find a nice apartment listed for $150 a night,

278
00:12:30,159 --> 00:12:31,240
and click checkout.

279
00:12:31,240 --> 00:12:34,879
Suddenly, the bill exploded to $400.

280
00:12:34,879 --> 00:12:39,519
$70 in platform service fees, $40 in occupancy taxes,

281
00:12:39,519 --> 00:12:42,200
and a staggering $180 cleaning fee.

282
00:12:42,200 --> 00:12:45,000
In the ultimate punchline, you unlock the front door

283
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:47,600
after an eight-hour flight, and sitting on the kitchen

284
00:12:47,600 --> 00:12:50,200
counter was a laminated chore list.

285
00:12:50,200 --> 00:12:54,320
Strip all the bed sheets, wash the linens, run the dishwasher,

286
00:12:54,320 --> 00:12:56,840
wipe down the baseboards, take out the trash,

287
00:12:56,840 --> 00:12:59,320
and drive your garbage bags four miles away

288
00:12:59,320 --> 00:13:01,159
to the local municipal dump.

289
00:13:01,159 --> 00:13:05,159
Guests looked at the $400 bill, looked at the mop in their hands,

290
00:13:05,159 --> 00:13:07,279
and realized the absurd truth.

291
00:13:07,279 --> 00:13:11,840
They were paying luxury hotel prices to do unpaid manual labor

292
00:13:11,840 --> 00:13:15,759
for an absentee landlord who wasn't even in the same state.

293
00:13:15,759 --> 00:13:17,600
Social media erupted.

294
00:13:17,600 --> 00:13:20,120
Millions of viral TikToks and tweets

295
00:13:20,120 --> 00:13:22,879
started posting the exact same headline.

296
00:13:22,879 --> 00:13:26,720
Hotels won because a hotel never hands you a mop,

297
00:13:26,720 --> 00:13:30,120
or charges you a hidden cleaning fee, just to sleep in their bed.

298
00:13:30,120 --> 00:13:32,200
Then came the government guillotine.

299
00:13:32,200 --> 00:13:36,000
Communities blamed short-term rentals for destroying neighborhoods,

300
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:38,639
and pricing local workers out of housing.

301
00:13:38,639 --> 00:13:43,320
In September 2023, New York City dropped a hammer with local lie

302
00:13:44,320 --> 00:13:47,919
making unhosted short-term rentals virtually illegal,

303
00:13:47,919 --> 00:13:53,279
and wiping out over 80% of Airbnb's New York listings overnight.

304
00:13:53,279 --> 00:13:57,960
Barcelona went even further announcing that by November 20,

305
00:13:57,960 --> 00:14:00,840
all 10,000 tourist departments in the city

306
00:14:00,840 --> 00:14:02,720
will be completely wiped out.

307
00:14:02,720 --> 00:14:05,480
Cities from Florence to Berlin slammed down

308
00:14:05,480 --> 00:14:08,720
aggressive 90-day caps and zoning bands.

309
00:14:08,720 --> 00:14:13,279
The company that started because a kid couldn't afford his rent in 2007.

310
00:14:13,799 --> 00:14:16,559
Was now accused of making rent unaffordable

311
00:14:16,559 --> 00:14:18,519
for an entire generation.

312
00:14:18,519 --> 00:14:20,039
That brings us to right now.

313
00:14:20,039 --> 00:14:23,480
Airbnb is still a massive financial machine.

314
00:14:23,480 --> 00:14:26,679
Pulling in over $12 billion in annual revenue,

315
00:14:26,679 --> 00:14:29,360
two and a half billion in net profit,

316
00:14:29,360 --> 00:14:33,440
and maintaining over 8 million active listings worldwide.

317
00:14:33,440 --> 00:14:37,200
But the explosive hyper-growth era is officially dead.

318
00:14:37,200 --> 00:14:41,120
Booking growth in mature markets has cooled to the single digits,

319
00:14:41,120 --> 00:14:43,480
and the brand spent three straight years,

320
00:14:43,480 --> 00:14:46,159
getting roasted on every corner of the internet.

321
00:14:46,159 --> 00:14:50,720
So how is Brian Cheskey responding in 2025 and 2026?

322
00:14:50,720 --> 00:14:54,799
By doing the one thing no proud founder ever wants to admit,

323
00:14:54,799 --> 00:14:57,480
he is desperately fixing his own machine.

324
00:14:57,480 --> 00:15:00,279
Airbnb rolled out total price display,

325
00:15:00,279 --> 00:15:03,679
so hidden cleaning fees can no longer be ambushed to check out.

326
00:15:03,679 --> 00:15:07,080
They banned hosts from demanding unreasonable chore lists

327
00:15:07,080 --> 00:15:09,000
like vacuuming and laundry,

328
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:12,519
and in the ultimate comedic irony in the history of tech.

329
00:15:12,519 --> 00:15:17,519
Airbnb's fastest growing category today is boutique hotels.

330
00:15:17,519 --> 00:15:22,240
The very industry the founders set out to destroy 18 years ago,

331
00:15:22,240 --> 00:15:25,440
with three cheap rubber mattresses on a living room floor,

332
00:15:25,440 --> 00:15:27,720
is now the exact thing they're relying on

333
00:15:27,720 --> 00:15:30,039
to keep their business alive.

334
00:15:30,039 --> 00:15:34,600
So what is the real actionable playbook you take from this 18 year war?

335
00:15:34,600 --> 00:15:37,120
If you strip away the billion dollar headlines

336
00:15:37,120 --> 00:15:41,240
and look at how this machine was actually constructed from zero,

337
00:15:41,240 --> 00:15:46,000
three brutal rules emerge that every entrepreneur must memorize.

338
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:49,320
First, borrow your distribution until you own it.

339
00:15:49,320 --> 00:15:52,960
Brian and Joe didn't spend a dime on Facebook or Google ads.

340
00:15:52,960 --> 00:15:57,039
They hijacked Craigslist 50 million users with a rogue script,

341
00:15:57,039 --> 00:16:00,840
and they turned a sold out conference into their personal billboard.

342
00:16:00,840 --> 00:16:02,600
If you have no marketing budget,

343
00:16:02,600 --> 00:16:05,279
look for where your customers are already congregating,

344
00:16:05,279 --> 00:16:08,799
and build an ethical bridge to siphon them over.

345
00:16:08,799 --> 00:16:13,240
Second, the answer is always in your user's kitchen, not in your code.

346
00:16:13,240 --> 00:16:16,799
When their revenue was flatlined at $200 a week,

347
00:16:16,799 --> 00:16:20,480
they didn't rewrite their algorithm or redesign their logo.

348
00:16:20,480 --> 00:16:21,799
They flew to Brooklyn,

349
00:16:21,799 --> 00:16:23,799
knocked on doors with a rented camera,

350
00:16:23,799 --> 00:16:25,559
drank tea with their hosts,

351
00:16:25,559 --> 00:16:28,879
and fixed the real friction, terrible photos,

352
00:16:28,879 --> 00:16:33,600
in the early days of any business, manual, unscalable legwork.

353
00:16:33,639 --> 00:16:39,080
We'll teach you more in 48 hours than six months of staring at Google Analytics.

354
00:16:39,080 --> 00:16:42,879
And third, never optimize the soul out of your product.

355
00:16:42,879 --> 00:16:46,200
Airbnb's genius was that it was cheaper, warmer,

356
00:16:46,200 --> 00:16:47,960
and more personal than hotel.

357
00:16:47,960 --> 00:16:51,120
But as soon as Wall Street demanded infinite growth,

358
00:16:51,120 --> 00:16:55,559
they allowed corporate hosts to charge $400 bait and switch fees

359
00:16:55,559 --> 00:16:58,240
and hand guests laminated chore lists.

360
00:16:58,240 --> 00:17:02,919
The moment your customer feels like they're being squeezed by a spreadsheet,

361
00:17:02,919 --> 00:17:05,000
instead of served by a human,

362
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:08,720
you create the exact crack your competitors will use to destroy you.

363
00:17:08,720 --> 00:17:12,200
Brian Cheskey didn't build $100 billion empire

364
00:17:12,200 --> 00:17:14,839
because he had a visionary master plan.

365
00:17:14,839 --> 00:17:17,720
He built it because he was resourceful enough

366
00:17:17,720 --> 00:17:20,680
to turn an inflatable mattress into rent money,

367
00:17:20,680 --> 00:17:25,240
stubborn enough to sell novelty cereal to pay off credit card debt,

368
00:17:25,240 --> 00:17:29,720
and humble enough to knock on strangers doors when his back was against the wall.

369
00:17:29,759 --> 00:17:33,720
You don't need a million dollars in seed capital to start building today.

370
00:17:33,720 --> 00:17:36,440
You just need to find a real, urgent problem.

371
00:17:36,440 --> 00:17:38,680
Look at the tools you already have in front of you

372
00:17:38,680 --> 00:17:43,839
and do the unglamorous work that nobody else is willing to do.

373
00:17:43,839 --> 00:17:48,720
So the next time you sit there thinking that every great business has already been built,

374
00:17:48,720 --> 00:17:49,799
look around the room.

375
00:17:49,799 --> 00:17:53,839
Every massive industry is hiding an annoying problem.

376
00:17:53,839 --> 00:17:56,400
Someone is desperately waiting for you to solve.

377
00:17:56,400 --> 00:18:00,400
And if you can't find one sitting at your desk, pack a bag and travel.

378
00:18:00,400 --> 00:18:03,319
Step outside your routine, notice what breaks,

379
00:18:03,319 --> 00:18:05,039
and see what frustrates people.

380
00:18:05,039 --> 00:18:08,279
It doesn't need to start with venture capital or a revolution.

381
00:18:08,279 --> 00:18:11,559
It could start with an $80 air bed on a living room rug

382
00:18:11,559 --> 00:18:13,759
and turn into $100 billion.

383
00:18:13,759 --> 00:18:15,359
What's in everyday annoyance?

384
00:18:15,359 --> 00:18:17,519
You think someone should turn into a business right now?

385
00:18:17,519 --> 00:18:19,319
Tell me in the comments down below.

386
00:18:19,319 --> 00:18:20,599
Stay curious.

387
00:18:20,599 --> 00:18:21,759
See you in the next one.
